  <office:body>
    <office:text>
      
  <text:p text:style-name="CoverSuperTag">ALSTOM TRANSPORT · RIYADH METRO PROJECT</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="CoverTitle">ShiftLine System Operations &amp; Manpower Allocation Guide</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="CoverSubtitle">Dual Roster Architecture, Capacity Balancing &amp; Work Order Execution Manual</text:p>
  
  <text:p text:style-name="CoverSpacerSmall"/>
  
    <text:p text:style-name="ImageContainer">
      <draw:frame draw:name="Img_3lagne" text:anchor-type="paragraph" svg:width="17.5cm" svg:height="0.85cm" draw:style-name="ImgFrameStyle">
        <draw:image>
          <office:binary-data></office:binary-data>
        </draw:image>
      </draw:frame>
    </text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="ImageCaption">Figure: Official Alstom SLV Header Banner &amp; Digital Board Integration</text:p>
  
  <text:p text:style-name="CoverSpacerSmall"/>

  <table:table table:name="CoverMetaTable" table:style-name="CoverMetaTableStyle">
    <table:table-column table:style-name="ColHalf"/>
    <table:table-column table:style-name="ColHalf"/>
    <table:table-row>
      <table:table-cell table:style-name="CoverMetaCell">
        <text:p text:style-name="CoverMetaLabel">TARGET INFRASTRUCTURE</text:p>
        <text:p text:style-name="CoverMetaVal">Riyadh Metro Lines 4, 5 &amp; 6 (SLV Maintenance)</text:p>
        <text:p text:style-name="CoverMetaLabel">TOTAL WORKFORCE MONITORED</text:p>
        <text:p text:style-name="CoverMetaVal">61 Technicians (22 Solo Pool L5 + 39 Combined L4/6)</text:p>
        <text:p text:style-name="CoverMetaLabel">WORK ORDERS ENGINE</text:p>
        <text:p text:style-name="CoverMetaVal">Maximo Monthly Planning Integration (316 Orders / 790 MP)</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
      <table:table-cell table:style-name="CoverMetaCell">
        <text:p text:style-name="CoverMetaLabel">DOCUMENT VERSION &amp; STATUS</text:p>
        <text:p text:style-name="CoverMetaVal">Version 2.4 — Official Operational Baseline</text:p>
        <text:p text:style-name="CoverMetaLabel">PUBLICATION &amp; RELEASE DATE</text:p>
        <text:p text:style-name="CoverMetaVal">October 2026 (Planning Cycle 2026–2027)</text:p>
        <text:p text:style-name="CoverMetaLabel">SECURITY &amp; COMPLIANCE LEVEL</text:p>
        <text:p text:style-name="CoverMetaVal">Alstom Restricted · Railway Safety Critical</text:p>
      </table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
  </table:table>

  <text:p text:style-name="CoverSpacerSmall"/>
  
    <table:table table:name="Callout_4qjsaa" table:style-name="CalloutBox">
      <table:table-column table:style-name="ColFullWidth"/>
      <table:table-row>
        <table:table-cell table:style-name="CalloutBoxCell">
          <text:p text:style-name="CalloutTitle">OPERATIONAL MANDATE</text:p>
          <text:p text:style-name="CalloutText">This comprehensive 30-page reference document provides maintenance directors, shift supervisors, lead engineers, and roster planners with the complete operational principles, visual guidelines, mathematical formulations, and daily execution workflows powering ShiftLine across the Riyadh SLV Metro maintenance network.</text:p>
        </table:table-cell>
      </table:table-row>
    </table:table>
  

<text:p text:style-name="PageBreak"/>

  
    <text:p text:style-name="SectionTag">SECTION I · EXECUTIVE OVERVIEW &amp; MISSION</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageTitle">02 · Executive Summary &amp; Operational Mission</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageSubtitle">Bridging 24/7 railway shift rosters with Maximo maintenance work order capacity</text:p>
  
  
  <text:p text:style-name="BodyText">ShiftLine is Alstom&apos;s custom-engineered intelligent roster and manpower capacity management platform, purpose-built for the Riyadh Metro Service Leader Vehicles (SLV) Maintenance Division covering Line 4 (Orange), Line 5 (Yellow), and Line 6 (Purple).</text:p>

  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• The Operational Challenge: </text:span>Balancing 61 specialized railway technicians across continuous round-the-clock shift cycles while simultaneously executing 316+ monthly preventive work orders under strict track possession time windows.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• The Traditional Bottleneck: </text:span>Legacy spreadsheet rosters operated in total isolation from work order backlogs, resulting in blind spot fatigue violations, severe night shift overloads, and last-minute track possession cancellations.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• The ShiftLine Solution: </text:span>A unified, real-time dual-engine platform combining an automated shift generation matrix (Planner) with an interactive Work Order Manpower balancing dashboard.</text:p>

  
    <table:table table:name="Callout_17idt9" table:style-name="CalloutBoxSuccess">
      <table:table-column table:style-name="ColFullWidth"/>
      <table:table-row>
        <table:table-cell table:style-name="CalloutBoxSuccessCell">
          <text:p text:style-name="CalloutTitleSuccess">CORE SYSTEM PRINCIPLE</text:p>
          <text:p text:style-name="CalloutText">Every maintenance work order requires qualified technician hours; every rostered technician represents finite operational capacity. ShiftLine calculates the exact net buffer in real time so supervisors never dispatch an under-crewed possession or violate fatigue rest limits.</text:p>
        </table:table-cell>
      </table:table-row>
    </table:table>
  

  <text:p text:style-name="BodyBold">Key Architectural Pillars of ShiftLine:</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Dual Pool Hierarchy: </text:span>Maintains dedicated independence for Line 5 (22 technicians) while managing a unified cross-line Combined Pool for Lines 4 &amp; 6 (39 technicians).</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Four Operational Shift Families: </text:span>Continuous coverage through Morning (M), Evening (E), Night (N), and General (GS) shifts with built-in 1-hour overlap windows for thorough shift handovers.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Smart Insights &amp; Deficit Engine: </text:span>Real-time validation flags staffing shortages, overtime breaches, and shift sequence violations before monthly publication.</text:p>

<text:p text:style-name="PageBreak"/>

  
    <text:p text:style-name="SectionTag">SECTION I · DOCUMENT STRUCTURE &amp; NAVIGATION</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageTitle">03 · Table of Contents &amp; Guide Navigation</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageSubtitle">Visual roadmap of the 30 chapters across four dedicated operational modules</text:p>
  
  
  <text:p text:style-name="BodyText">This operational manual is organized into four core functional sections. Maintenance planners and supervisors should refer directly to the relevant chapters based on their operational phase:</text:p>

  
    <table:table table:name="Table_ksvvrf" table:style-name="CustomDataTable">
      <table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_4_0"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_3_0"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_6_0"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_4_5"/>
      <table:table-header-rows><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Section</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Chapter Range</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Primary Functional Area</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Key Stakeholders</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row></table:table-header-rows>
      <table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Section I: Architecture &amp; Foundations</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Pages 01 – 05</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Lines, Pools, Shift Families &amp; Operating Rules</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Maintenance Directors, Planners</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Section II: The Planner Module</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Pages 06 – 16</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">31-Day Roster Matrix, Auto-Generation &amp; Insights</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Shift Supervisors, Roster Clerks</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Section III: Work Orders &amp; Manpower</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Pages 17 – 28</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Maximo Import, Shift Capacity &amp; Deficit Analysis</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Work Planners, Dispatch Engineers</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Section IV: Dispatch, Audit &amp; Checklists</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Pages 29 – 30</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Crew Locking, ERP Export &amp; Daily Audit Steps</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Operations Lead, Safety Officers</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row>
    </table:table>
  

  <text:p text:style-name="CoverSpacerSmall"/>
  <text:p text:style-name="BodyBold">Detailed Chapter Navigation Matrix:</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Pages 04-05: </text:span>Pool Architecture (Solo vs Combined) and 24/7 Railway Shift Timings &amp; Overlaps.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Pages 06-08: </text:span>Planner User Interface, Matrix Anatomy, and Shift Palette Brush Mode.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Pages 09-11: </text:span>Staff Rules, Priority Hierarchy, Auto-Generation Engine, and Fatigue Compliance.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Pages 12-14: </text:span>Live Headcount Bar, Shortfall Error Detection (11 vs 38 errors), and One-Click Auto-Cover.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Pages 15-16: </text:span>Leave Types, Saudi Labour Law 48-Hour Thresholds, and Excel Multi-Sheet Import.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Pages 17-21: </text:span>Work Orders Overview, Yellow Columns Dictionary, Dashboard Metrics &amp; Crew Intensity.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Pages 22-25: </text:span>Railway Shift Classification, Status Tracking, Capacity Cards &amp; Severe Overload Analysis.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Pages 26-28: </text:span>Date Timeline Navigation, Cross-Line Sharing, and Multi-Column Filtering.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Pages 29-30: </text:span>Specialist Crew Locking, Handover Protocol, and Monthly Audit Compliance Checklists.</text:p>

<text:p text:style-name="PageBreak"/>

  
    <text:p text:style-name="SectionTag">SECTION I · LINE &amp; POOL HIERARCHY</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageTitle">04 · Line &amp; Pool Hierarchy: Combined vs Solo Pools</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageSubtitle">Organizational allocation of 61 maintenance technicians across Riyadh Metro</text:p>
  
  
  <text:p text:style-name="BodyText">Riyadh Metro SLV Maintenance operates with two distinct organizational models to balance physical depot constraints with operational agility across Lines 4, 5, and 6:</text:p>

  
    <table:table table:name="Table_ablict" table:style-name="CustomDataTable">
      <table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_4_0"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_3_5"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_2_5"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_3_0"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_4_5"/>
      <table:table-header-rows><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Pool Structure</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Covered Lines</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Total Staff</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Depot Location</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Operating Model &amp; Mobility</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row></table:table-header-rows>
      <table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Line 5 Solo Pool</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Line 5 (Yellow Line)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">22 Technicians</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Depot 5 (Dedicated)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Strictly segregated technicians dedicated solely to Line 5 rolling stock and wayside assets.</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Lines 4 &amp; 6 Combined Pool</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Line 4 (Orange) &amp; Line 6 (Purple)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">39 Technicians</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Shared Depot 4/6</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Unified multi-skilled crew pool dynamically allocated across Lines 4 and 6 based on possession workload.</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row>
    </table:table>
  

  <text:p text:style-name="CoverSpacerSmall"/>
  <text:p text:style-name="BodyBold">Why Line 5 Operates as an Independent Solo Pool:</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Geographical Separation: </text:span>Line 5 operates underground along King Abdulaziz Road with its own independent maintenance depot and specialized rolling stock configuration.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Dedicated Fleet Focus: </text:span>Prevents cross-contamination of shift hours and ensures dedicated technicians maintain deep familiarity with Line 5 wayside signalling.</text:p>

  <text:p text:style-name="CoverSpacerSmall"/>
  <text:p text:style-name="BodyBold">Why Lines 4 &amp; 6 Operate as a Combined Pool:</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Shared Infrastructure Synergy: </text:span>Lines 4 and 6 share common track junctions, rolling stock platforms, and a centralized maintenance facility.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Dynamic Manpower Absorption: </text:span>A surge in heavy track grinding on Line 4 can be absorbed by Line 6 technicians without requiring external contractors or overtime exhaustion.</text:p>

  
    <table:table table:name="Callout_12ieqy" table:style-name="CalloutBox">
      <table:table-column table:style-name="ColFullWidth"/>
      <table:table-row>
        <table:table-cell table:style-name="CalloutBoxCell">
          <text:p text:style-name="CalloutTitle">POINTER IN SHIFTLINE UI</text:p>
          <text:p text:style-name="CalloutText">In the top toolbar of both Planner and Work Orders, planners can instantaneously toggle between &quot;Line 5&quot; and &quot;Line 4 &amp; 6&quot;. The active roster, headcount bars, and capacity cards immediately re-render to reflect the selected pool.</text:p>
        </table:table-cell>
      </table:table-row>
    </table:table>
  

<text:p text:style-name="PageBreak"/>

  
    <text:p text:style-name="SectionTag">SECTION I · SHIFT TIMINGS &amp; OVERLAPS</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageTitle">05 · Shift Timings &amp; Railway Operational Families</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageSubtitle">24-hour round-the-clock coverage with seamless 1-hour handover windows</text:p>
  
  
  <text:p text:style-name="BodyText">Railway maintenance requires unbroken round-the-clock vigilance. ShiftLine structures all technician schedules into four standardized operational shift families:</text:p>

  
    <table:table table:name="Table_ag1mjo" table:style-name="CustomDataTable">
      <table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_2_0"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_2_5"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_2_5"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_2_5"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_8_0"/>
      <table:table-header-rows><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Shift Code</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Shift Name</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Working Hours</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Duration</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Primary Maintenance Activities &amp; Railway Focus</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row></table:table-header-rows>
      <table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">M</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Morning Shift</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">06:00 – 15:00</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">9.0 hrs (incl 1h break)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Station DCS monitoring, passenger service support, first-response corrective maintenance.</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">E</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Evening Shift</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">14:00 – 23:00</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">9.0 hrs (incl 1h break)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Station facilities, lighting, platform doors, pre-possession tool staging and safety briefing.</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">N</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Night Shift</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">22:00 – 07:00</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">9.0 hrs (incl 1h break)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Mainline track possessions, power isolation, Cat-3/4 heavy preventive maintenance, signalling testing.</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">GS</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">General Shift</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">08:00 – 17:00</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">9.0 hrs (incl 1h break)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Workshop overhaul, component repairs, logistics, quality audits, tool calibration.</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row>
    </table:table>
  

  <text:p text:style-name="CoverSpacerSmall"/>
  <text:p text:style-name="BodyBold">The 1-Hour Handover Overlap Philosophy:</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Morning to Evening Overlap (14:00 – 15:00): </text:span>Outgoing morning technicians conduct physical tool audits and brief incoming evening crews on active faults occurring during morning peak passenger hours.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Evening to Night Overlap (22:00 – 23:00): </text:span>Critical safety briefing window: Evening crews hand over track possession clearances, traction power isolation certificates, and track safety equipment to the incoming night possession crews.</text:p>

  
    <table:table table:name="Callout_em8jhg" table:style-name="CalloutBoxWarning">
      <table:table-column table:style-name="ColFullWidth"/>
      <table:table-row>
        <table:table-cell table:style-name="CalloutBoxWarningCell">
          <text:p text:style-name="CalloutTitleWarning">SAFETY CRITICAL RULE</text:p>
          <text:p text:style-name="CalloutText">No technician scheduled on Night Shift (N: 22:00–07:00) may be scheduled on Morning Shift (M: 06:00–15:00) the following day. A mandatory minimum 24-hour rest window is enforced by the auto-generation engine.</text:p>
        </table:table-cell>
      </table:table-row>
    </table:table>
  

<text:p text:style-name="PageBreak"/>

  
    <text:p text:style-name="SectionTag">SECTION II · PLANNER MODULE OVERVIEW</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageTitle">06 · Planner Interface &amp; Command Toolbar</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageSubtitle">The nerve center for viewing, generating, and fine-tuning monthly rosters</text:p>
  
  
  <text:p text:style-name="BodyText">The ShiftLine Planner interface provides an end-to-end command dashboard designed for rapid month-long roster visualization, rule compliance, and manual fine-tuning:</text:p>

  
    <text:p text:style-name="ImageContainer">
      <draw:frame draw:name="Img_4fm8yr" text:anchor-type="paragraph" svg:width="17.5cm" svg:height="7.8cm" draw:style-name="ImgFrameStyle">
        <draw:image>
          <office:binary-data></office:binary-data>
        </draw:image>
      </draw:frame>
    </text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="ImageCaption">Figure: ShiftLine Planner Grid: Line 5 (September 2026, 22 Staff, 11 Shortfall Warnings)</text:p>
  

  <text:p text:style-name="BodyBold">Core Components of the Planner Command Bar:</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Month &amp; Year Picker: </text:span>Effortlessly switch between active planning periods (e.g., September 2026, November 2026). State is persisted across browser refreshes.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Pool Switcher Tabs: </text:span>One-click toggle between Line 5 Solo Pool (22 staff) and Lines 4 &amp; 6 Combined Pool (39 staff).</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Auto-Generate Roster Button: </text:span>Executes the 5-tier intelligent scheduling engine to balance shifts against staff rules in under 1 second.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Interactive Brush Toggle: </text:span>Activates &quot;Paint Brush&quot; mode for ultra-rapid cell assignment without opening repetitive dialogs.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Import Excel &amp; Export Actions: </text:span>Facilitates seamless two-way interoperability with legacy Alstom operational spreadsheets.</text:p>

<text:p text:style-name="PageBreak"/>

  
    <text:p text:style-name="SectionTag">SECTION II · ROSTER GRID ANATOMY</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageTitle">07 · Roster Grid Anatomy &amp; Technician Matrix</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageSubtitle">Structure of the 31-day technician scheduling matrix</text:p>
  
  
  <text:p text:style-name="BodyText">The central grid organizes technicians along the vertical axis and days of the month along the horizontal axis, providing total situational awareness:</text:p>

  
    <text:p text:style-name="ImageContainer">
      <draw:frame draw:name="Img_e7tsya" text:anchor-type="paragraph" svg:width="17.5cm" svg:height="7.8cm" draw:style-name="ImgFrameStyle">
        <draw:image>
          <office:binary-data></office:binary-data>
        </draw:image>
      </draw:frame>
    </text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="ImageCaption">Figure: ShiftLine Planner Grid: Lines 4 &amp; 6 (September 2026, 39 Staff, 38 Shortfall Warnings)</text:p>
  

  <text:p text:style-name="BodyBold">Anatomy of Grid Columns and Elements:</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Staff Identification Header (Sticky Left): </text:span>Displays technician Full Name, unique Alstom Employee ID, designated Primary Trade (Signalling, Track, Telecom), and primary line assignment.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Day Columns (1 to 28/30/31): </text:span>Each day column displays the day number and weekday abbreviation (e.g., &quot;1 Tue&quot;, &quot;4 Fri&quot;). Weekend rest days (Friday/Saturday) are visually highlighted.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Monthly Summary Totals (Sticky Right): </text:span>Live recalculation of Working Shifts (M+E+N+GS), Scheduled Rest Days (OFF), Approved Leaves (AL+SL), and Total Accumulated Working Hours.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Interactive Shift Cell: </text:span>Displays current shift code with color coding. Clicking a cell opens the shift selector or paints the active palette brush code.</text:p>

<text:p text:style-name="PageBreak"/>

  
    <text:p text:style-name="SectionTag">SECTION II · SHIFT PALETTE &amp; BRUSH MODE</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageTitle">08 · Shift Code Palette &amp; Interactive Brush Tool</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageSubtitle">Rapid single-click roster editing and visual shift coding standards</text:p>
  
  
  <text:p text:style-name="BodyText">To minimize administrative overhead, ShiftLine introduces the &quot;Interactive Shift Brush&quot;, allowing supervisors to paint shift assignments across days and technicians instantaneously:</text:p>

  
    <table:table table:name="Table_za7ba" table:style-name="CustomDataTable">
      <table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_1_8"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_3_5"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_4_2"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_8_0"/>
      <table:table-header-rows><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Code</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Visual Badge Color</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Shift Description</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Typical Duty Scope &amp; Application</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row></table:table-header-rows>
      <table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">M</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Emerald Green (#10b981)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Morning Shift (06:00–15:00)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Station maintenance, DCS, corrective peak passenger support.</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">E</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Royal Blue (#2563eb)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Evening Shift (14:00–23:00)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Platform screen doors, station facilities, pre-night prep.</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">N</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Indigo / Purple (#6366f1)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Night Shift (22:00–07:00)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Mainline track possessions, rail grinding, interlocking testing.</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">GS</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Amber / Orange (#f59e0b)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">General Shift (08:00–17:00)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Workshop overhauls, engineering office, training, logistics.</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">OFF</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Neutral Slate (#64748b)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Scheduled Rest Day</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Mandatory 48-hour weekly rest window (Fri/Sat or Wed/Thu).</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">AL</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Rose / Red (#f43f5e)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Annual Leave</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Approved paid annual vacation entitlement.</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">SL / TR</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Purple / Cyan (#a855f7)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Sick Leave / Training</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Certified medical absence or technical training academy.</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row>
    </table:table>
  

  <text:p text:style-name="CoverSpacerSmall"/>
  <text:p text:style-name="BodyBold">How Interactive Brush Mode Operates:</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Step 1: Select Shift Code: </text:span>Click any code button in the top palette (e.g., click &quot;N&quot; for Night Shift). The button highlights with an active glowing outline.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Step 2: Paint the Grid: </text:span>Click across any cells in the roster grid. Each clicked cell immediately updates to the selected shift code without requiring confirmation.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Step 3: Instant Recalculation: </text:span>The bottom Headcount Bar, Insights Panel, and technician monthly totals recalculate in real time as each cell is painted.</text:p>

  
    <table:table table:name="Callout_jnfre" table:style-name="CalloutBox">
      <table:table-column table:style-name="ColFullWidth"/>
      <table:table-row>
        <table:table-cell table:style-name="CalloutBoxCell">
          <text:p text:style-name="CalloutTitle">PRO TIP</text:p>
          <text:p text:style-name="CalloutText">To quickly assign a block of rest days, select &quot;OFF&quot; on the brush palette and click across Friday and Saturday for the entire maintenance gang in seconds.</text:p>
        </table:table-cell>
      </table:table-row>
    </table:table>
  

<text:p text:style-name="PageBreak"/>

  
    <text:p text:style-name="SectionTag">SECTION II · TECHNICIAN RULES &amp; REST PAIRS</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageTitle">09 · Technician Rules, Defaults &amp; Rest Day Pairs</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageSubtitle">Configuring base technician profiles and Saudi Labour Law rest day pairings</text:p>
  
  
  <text:p text:style-name="BodyText">Every technician profile in ShiftLine contains operational parameters that govern how shifts and rest days are automatically generated:</text:p>

  
    <text:p text:style-name="ImageContainer">
      <draw:frame draw:name="Img_vp0ds3" text:anchor-type="paragraph" svg:width="9.5cm" svg:height="8.5cm" draw:style-name="ImgFrameStyle">
        <draw:image>
          <office:binary-data></office:binary-data>
        </draw:image>
      </draw:frame>
    </text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="ImageCaption">Figure: Staff Operational Rules &amp; Shift Timings Configuration Dialog</text:p>
  

  <text:p text:style-name="BodyBold">Mandatory Rest Day Pairing Configurations:</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Pair A: Friday &amp; Saturday (Standard Weekend Gang): </text:span>Assigned to 60% of the maintenance workforce to align with regional weekend customs and depot support schedules.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Pair B: Wednesday &amp; Thursday (Mid-Week Maintenance Gang): </text:span>Ensures robust technician coverage on weekends when passenger train service is intense.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Pair C: Sunday &amp; Monday (Depot Overhaul Gang): </text:span>Dedicated workshop technicians handling preventative component overhauls.</text:p>

  
    <table:table table:name="Callout_jdezf8" table:style-name="CalloutBoxWarning">
      <table:table-column table:style-name="ColFullWidth"/>
      <table:table-row>
        <table:table-cell table:style-name="CalloutBoxWarningCell">
          <text:p text:style-name="CalloutTitleWarning">LABOUR REGULATION COMPLIANCE</text:p>
          <text:p text:style-name="CalloutText">Saudi Labour Law Article 98 requires two consecutive rest days (48 hours) for every five consecutive working days. ShiftLine enforces rest days strictly in pairs and alerts planners if single disjointed rest days are assigned.</text:p>
        </table:table-cell>
      </table:table-row>
    </table:table>
  

<text:p text:style-name="PageBreak"/>

  
    <text:p text:style-name="SectionTag">SECTION II · AUTO-GENERATION PRIORITY ENGINE</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageTitle">10 · The Auto-Generation Engine &amp; 5-Tier Priority Rules</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageSubtitle">Algorithmic hierarchy governing automatic monthly roster generation</text:p>
  
  
  <text:p text:style-name="BodyText">When a planner clicks &quot;Auto-Generate Roster&quot;, ShiftLine runs a deterministic 5-tier constraint solver to populate the 31-day grid without human error:</text:p>

  
    <table:table table:name="Table_g71wsp" table:style-name="CustomDataTable">
      <table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_3_0"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_3_5"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_8_0"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_3_0"/>
      <table:table-header-rows><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Priority Tier</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Rule Type</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Precedence Behavior</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Can Be Overwritten?</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row></table:table-header-rows>
      <table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Tier 1 (Highest)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Manual Overrides &amp; Locked Cells</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Any shift cell specifically painted or locked by the supervisor is permanently preserved.</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">NO — Absolute Precedence</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Tier 2</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Approved Leaves (AL, SL, TR)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Technicians on approved leave are scheduled on leave code; no work shifts can be injected.</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">NO — Protected Status</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Tier 3</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Mandatory Rest Day Pairs</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Rest day pairs (e.g. Fri/Sat) are assigned according to the employee profile to ensure 48h rest.</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Only by Tier 1 Overrides</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Tier 4</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Forward Cyclic Rotation</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Populates working days in cyclic sequence (M → E → N) while checking minimum headcount needs.</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Yes — Adjusted for Coverage</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Tier 5 (Fallback)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Default Shift Profile</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Fills any remaining unassigned working days with the technician&apos;s baseline contract shift.</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Default Baseline</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row>
    </table:table>
  

  <text:p text:style-name="CoverSpacerSmall"/>
  <text:p text:style-name="BodyBold">Step-by-Step Generation Execution Flow:</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Phase 1: Lockdown &amp; Leave Staging: </text:span>The engine preserves all locked shifts and paints approved annual leave dates across the 31-day horizon.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Phase 2: Rest Day Anchoring: </text:span>The technician&apos;s assigned rest day pair is repeated across all 4 weeks of the month.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Phase 3: Shift Allocation &amp; Balancing: </text:span>Working days are populated, continuously balancing the daily headcount bar against minimum required staffing.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Phase 4: Sanity &amp; Conflict Pass: </text:span>The completed grid is audited for fatigue violations (e.g., Night shift immediately followed by Morning shift).</text:p>

<text:p text:style-name="PageBreak"/>

  
    <text:p text:style-name="SectionTag">SECTION II · ROTATION &amp; FATIGUE COMPLIANCE</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageTitle">11 · Auto-Rotation &amp; Fatigue Management</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageSubtitle">Biomedical circadian rhythm protection and railway fatigue limits</text:p>
  
  
  <text:p text:style-name="BodyText">Railway maintenance is safety-critical. A fatigued technician operating an overhead catenary vehicle or testing an interlocking switch presents unacceptable risks. ShiftLine enforces strict circadian safety logic:</text:p>

  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Forward-Rotating Shift Sequences: </text:span>Shift sequences must always rotate forward in time: Morning (06:00) → Evening (14:00) → Night (22:00) → Rest Days (OFF). Forward rotation follows human circadian biology.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Strict Prohibition of Backward Rotation: </text:span>The dangerous sequence of Night Shift (ends at 07:00) into Morning Shift (starts at 06:00 next day) is prohibited because it provides only 23 hours of elapsed time between shifts.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Night Shift Ceiling (Maximum 4 Consecutive Nights): </text:span>Technicians may not be scheduled for more than 4 consecutive night shifts. On the 5th day, the engine forces a mandatory 48-hour rest block.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Equitable Weekend Distribution: </text:span>Over a 90-day rolling cycle, the auto-balancer ensures all technicians rotate equitably through weekend shifts rather than burdening the same junior technicians.</text:p>

  
    <table:table table:name="Callout_68nefr" table:style-name="CalloutBoxWarning">
      <table:table-column table:style-name="ColFullWidth"/>
      <table:table-row>
        <table:table-cell table:style-name="CalloutBoxWarningCell">
          <text:p text:style-name="CalloutTitleWarning">FATIGUE AUDIT LOG</text:p>
          <text:p text:style-name="CalloutText">If a supervisor manually forces a backward rotation (e.g., painting &quot;M&quot; directly following &quot;N&quot;), ShiftLine accepts the override but immediately displays a bright red fatigue violation badge in the Insights Panel.</text:p>
        </table:table-cell>
      </table:table-row>
    </table:table>
  

  <text:p text:style-name="CoverSpacerSmall"/>
  <text:p text:style-name="BodyBold">Recommended Circadian 28-Day Shift Cycle Pattern:</text:p>
  
    <table:table table:name="Table_21hk7ek" table:style-name="CustomDataTable">
      <table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_2_5"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_2_1"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_2_1"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_2_1"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_2_1"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_2_3"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_2_3"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_2_1"/>
      <table:table-header-rows><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Week</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Monday</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Tuesday</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Wednesday</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Thursday</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Friday</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Saturday</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Sunday</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row></table:table-header-rows>
      <table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Week 1</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Morning (M)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Morning (M)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Morning (M)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Morning (M)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">REST (OFF)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">REST (OFF)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Morning (M)</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Week 2</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Evening (E)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Evening (E)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Evening (E)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Evening (E)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">REST (OFF)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">REST (OFF)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Evening (E)</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Week 3</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Night (N)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Night (N)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Night (N)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Night (N)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">REST (OFF)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">REST (OFF)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Night (N)</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Week 4</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">General (GS)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">General (GS)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">General (GS)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">General (GS)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">REST (OFF)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">REST (OFF)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">General (GS)</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row>
    </table:table>
  

<text:p text:style-name="PageBreak"/>

  
    <text:p text:style-name="SectionTag">SECTION II · LIVE HEADCOUNT BAR</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageTitle">12 · Live Headcount Bar &amp; Minimum Thresholds</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageSubtitle">Real-time daily staffing metrics and minimum threshold monitoring</text:p>
  
  
  <text:p text:style-name="BodyText">Positioned directly beneath the 31-day roster grid is the Live Headcount Bar, continuously recalculating available staffing for every single day and shift:</text:p>

  
    <text:p text:style-name="ImageContainer">
      <draw:frame draw:name="Img_xua93o" text:anchor-type="paragraph" svg:width="17.5cm" svg:height="3.2cm" draw:style-name="ImgFrameStyle">
        <draw:image>
          <office:binary-data></office:binary-data>
        </draw:image>
      </draw:frame>
    </text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="ImageCaption">Figure: Live Headcount Bar showing Morning, Evening, and Night totals per day</text:p>
  

  <text:p text:style-name="BodyBold">Minimum Shift Headcount Requirements by Pool:</text:p>
  
    <table:table table:name="Table_p6c0va" table:style-name="CustomDataTable">
      <table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_3_5"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_4_5"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_5_0"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_4_5"/>
      <table:table-header-rows><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Operational Shift</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Line 5 Solo Pool (22 Staff)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Lines 4 &amp; 6 Combined Pool (39 Staff)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Operational Failure Consequence</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row></table:table-header-rows>
      <table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Morning (M)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Minimum 4 Technicians</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Minimum 7 Technicians</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Inability to staff station first-response teams during peak passenger hours.</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Evening (E)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Minimum 3 Technicians</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Minimum 6 Technicians</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Delays in staging tools and issuing track possession safety certificates.</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Night (N)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Minimum 5 Technicians</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Minimum 10 Technicians</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Mandatory cancellation of mainline track possessions due to insufficient track safety gang.</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row>
    </table:table>
  

  <text:p text:style-name="CoverSpacerSmall"/>
  <text:p text:style-name="BodyBold">Visual Status Color Codes:</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Green Badge (Optimal): </text:span>Assigned staffing equals or exceeds the required threshold with adequate safety margin.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Yellow Badge (Tight): </text:span>Staffing exactly matches the minimum threshold. Any unexpected sick call will trigger a shortfall.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Red Badge (Shortfall Alert): </text:span>Staffing is below the required threshold. Triggers an entry in the Insights Panel.</text:p>

<text:p text:style-name="PageBreak"/>

  
    <text:p text:style-name="SectionTag">SECTION II · INSIGHTS PANEL &amp; SHORTFALLS</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageTitle">13 · Insights Panel &amp; Shortfall Detection Engine</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageSubtitle">Instant identification of staffing deficits across 31 calendar days</text:p>
  
  
  <text:p text:style-name="BodyText">The Insights Panel provides maintenance planners with an automated discrepancy report, listing every instance where assigned staffing fails to meet operational thresholds:</text:p>

  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Real-World Case Study: Line 5 Solo Pool (September 2026): </text:span>The Insights Panel flagged exactly 11 shortfall errors in Line 5. Most occurred on Thursday nights where leave clustering reduced available night staff from the required 5 down to 3.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Real-World Case Study: Lines 4 &amp; 6 Combined Pool (September 2026): </text:span>The Insights Panel flagged 38 shortfall errors across Lines 4 &amp; 6 due to 7 technicians being simultaneously scheduled for Annual Leave during school holidays.</text:p>

  
    <table:table table:name="Callout_rr58pq" table:style-name="CalloutBox">
      <table:table-column table:style-name="ColFullWidth"/>
      <table:table-row>
        <table:table-cell table:style-name="CalloutBoxCell">
          <text:p text:style-name="CalloutTitle">INSIGHT ITEM BREAKDOWN</text:p>
          <text:p text:style-name="CalloutText">Each error entry in the Insights Panel explicitly details: [Date] + [Shift Type] + [Assigned vs Required Headcount] + [Deficit Count]. Clicking any error entry automatically scrolls the grid directly to that day and shift.</text:p>
        </table:table-cell>
      </table:table-row>
    </table:table>
  

  <text:p text:style-name="CoverSpacerSmall"/>
  <text:p text:style-name="BodyBold">Typical Causes of Operational Shortfalls:</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Leave Clustering: </text:span>Multiple technicians in the same trade (e.g., three Interlocking Specialists) taking annual leave simultaneously.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Rest Day Congestion: </text:span>Over-allocating staff to Friday/Saturday rest pairs, leaving mid-week night shifts vulnerable.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Unbalanced Rotation Transitions: </text:span>Technicians transitioning from Night to Rest leaving a 24-hour vacuum in coverage.</text:p>

<text:p text:style-name="PageBreak"/>

  
    <text:p text:style-name="SectionTag">SECTION II · ONE-CLICK SHORTFALL RESOLUTION</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageTitle">14 · One-Click Shortfall Resolution Engine</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageSubtitle">Intelligent automated candidate suggestion to eliminate roster deficits</text:p>
  
  
  <text:p text:style-name="BodyText">Rather than forcing supervisors to manually scan 61 rows to find available technicians, ShiftLine features a smart candidate suggestion engine accessible directly from the Insights Panel:</text:p>

  <text:p text:style-name="BodyBold">How the &quot;Show Who Can Cover&quot; Algorithm Works:</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Criterion 1: Availability Check: </text:span>The algorithm scans all technicians who are currently scheduled on &quot;OFF&quot; (Rest Day) or &quot;GS&quot; (General Shift) on the shortfall date.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Criterion 2: Fatigue &amp; Rest Interval Validation: </text:span>It verifies that assigning the shift will not violate the mandatory 11-hour rest interval before their next shift or create a backward rotation.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Criterion 3: Trade &amp; Skill Matching: </text:span>It prioritizes technicians holding the exact trade certification required by the short-staffed shift (e.g. Signalling vs Track).</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Criterion 4: Overtime Balancing: </text:span>It ranks eligible candidates by lowest accumulated overtime hours during the month to ensure fair overtime distribution.</text:p>

  
    <table:table table:name="Callout_f0o8j" table:style-name="CalloutBoxSuccess">
      <table:table-column table:style-name="ColFullWidth"/>
      <table:table-row>
        <table:table-cell table:style-name="CalloutBoxSuccessCell">
          <text:p text:style-name="CalloutTitleSuccess">ONE-CLICK ACTION</text:p>
          <text:p text:style-name="CalloutText">Clicking &quot;Assign &amp; Resolve&quot; beside any suggested candidate immediately updates the roster cell, converts their schedule to Extra Duty, removes the error from the Insights Panel, and turns the headcount badge green.</text:p>
        </table:table-cell>
      </table:table-row>
    </table:table>
  

  <text:p text:style-name="CoverSpacerSmall"/>
  <text:p text:style-name="BodyBold">Resolution Action Matrix:</text:p>
  
    <table:table table:name="Table_30227" table:style-name="CustomDataTable">
      <table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_4_0"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_3_5"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_5_5"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_4_5"/>
      <table:table-header-rows><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Original Status</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Target Shift</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Resolution Action</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Compensation Impact</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row></table:table-header-rows>
      <table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">OFF (Rest Day)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Night Shift (N)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Candidate called in for Night Duty</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Extra Duty (Overtime at 1.5x hourly rate)</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">GS (08:00–17:00)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Evening Shift (E)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Shift rescheduled from GS to E</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Regular shift change (0 overtime, schedule swap)</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">GS (08:00–17:00)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Night Shift (N)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Shift shifted with 24h rest prep</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Regular shift change + night differential allowance</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row>
    </table:table>
  

<text:p text:style-name="PageBreak"/>

  
    <text:p text:style-name="SectionTag">SECTION II · LEAVE &amp; LABOUR LAW COMPLIANCE</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageTitle">15 · Leave Management &amp; Labour Law Compliance</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageSubtitle">Tracking statutory leaves, extra duty hours, and Saudi Labour Law limits</text:p>
  
  
  <text:p text:style-name="BodyText">ShiftLine maintains strict adherence to Saudi Labour Law (Royal Decree M/51) regarding maximum working hours, overtime compensation, and statutory leave allowances:</text:p>

  
    <text:p text:style-name="ImageContainer">
      <draw:frame draw:name="Img_6w5njl" text:anchor-type="paragraph" svg:width="17.5cm" svg:height="2.8cm" draw:style-name="ImgFrameStyle">
        <draw:image>
          <office:binary-data></office:binary-data>
        </draw:image>
      </draw:frame>
    </text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="ImageCaption">Figure: Technician Monthly Working Hours and Shift Summary Cards</text:p>
  

  <text:p text:style-name="BodyBold">Key Labour Law Parameters Enforced by ShiftLine:</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Standard Working Week Ceiling (Article 98): </text:span>Maximum 48 regular working hours per week (8 hours per day across 6 working days, or 9.6 hours across 5 working days). ShiftLine flags any technician exceeding 48 hours without authorized overtime.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Ramadan Reduced Working Hours: </text:span>ShiftLine includes an automatic Ramadan mode reducing maximum weekly working hours for Muslim staff to 36 hours (6 hours per day).</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Overtime Compensation Tracking (Article 107): </text:span>Any hours worked beyond baseline schedule or on designated rest days are logged into the &quot;Extra Duty&quot; column and tagged for 150% overtime payroll compensation.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Statutory Annual Leave (Article 109): </text:span>Technicians are entitled to a minimum of 21 calendar days (increasing to 30 days after 5 continuous years). ShiftLine maintains a running balance.</text:p>

  
    <table:table table:name="Callout_jn5bji" table:style-name="CalloutBox">
      <table:table-column table:style-name="ColFullWidth"/>
      <table:table-row>
        <table:table-cell table:style-name="CalloutBoxCell">
          <text:p text:style-name="CalloutTitle">PAYROLL EXPORT SAFETY</text:p>
          <text:p text:style-name="CalloutText">ShiftLine prevents unauthorized overtime by requiring supervisor authentication before any &quot;Extra Duty&quot; shift is exported to Alstom&apos;s SAP HR payroll integration.</text:p>
        </table:table-cell>
      </table:table-row>
    </table:table>
  

<text:p text:style-name="PageBreak"/>

  
    <text:p text:style-name="SectionTag">SECTION II · EXCEL ROSTER IMPORT</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageTitle">16 · Excel Roster Import &amp; Multi-Sheet Architecture</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageSubtitle">Importing legacy master rosters with multi-sheet detection and data validation</text:p>
  
  
  <text:p text:style-name="BodyText">ShiftLine provides full bidirectional compatibility with Alstom&apos;s master operational spreadsheets through an intelligent Excel import pipeline:</text:p>

  
    <text:p text:style-name="ImageContainer">
      <draw:frame draw:name="Img_2vknxe" text:anchor-type="paragraph" svg:width="11.5cm" svg:height="7.5cm" draw:style-name="ImgFrameStyle">
        <draw:image>
          <office:binary-data></office:binary-data>
        </draw:image>
      </draw:frame>
    </text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="ImageCaption">Figure: Sheet Picker Modal: Detecting &quot;Line 5&quot; and &quot;Line 4 &amp; 6&quot; sheets</text:p>
  

  <text:p text:style-name="BodyBold">The 4-Step Excel Import Protocol:</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Step 1: Upload File: </text:span>Drag and drop or select the Alstom monthly Excel workbook (e.g. &quot;November Monthly Planning 1_ Line 5 _ 4&amp;6.xlsx&quot;).</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Step 2: Sheet Detection: </text:span>The workbook parser inspects all sheets. If separate sheets exist for &quot;Line 5&quot; and &quot;Line 4 &amp; 6&quot;, the interactive Sheet Picker modal prompts the user to select the target sheet.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Step 3: Column Auto-Mapping: </text:span>The parser automatically identifies employee name columns, Alstom ID columns, and day columns 1 through 31, normalizing non-standard headers.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Step 4: Shift Code Normalization &amp; Validation: </text:span>Shift codes are converted to uppercase, whitespace is trimmed, and invalid codes are highlighted for correction before the database is committed.</text:p>

<text:p text:style-name="PageBreak"/>

  
    <text:p text:style-name="SectionTag">SECTION III · WORK ORDERS &amp; MANPOWER MODULE</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageTitle">17 · Work Orders &amp; Manpower Module Overview</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageSubtitle">Bridging Maximo enterprise work orders with live technician capacity</text:p>
  
  
  <text:p text:style-name="BodyText">The Work Orders &amp; Manpower Module is ShiftLine&apos;s operational bridge connecting enterprise maintenance work orders (from IBM Maximo or SAP PM) directly to available technician capacity:</text:p>

  
    <text:p text:style-name="ImageContainer">
      <draw:frame draw:name="Img_5d999w" text:anchor-type="paragraph" svg:width="17.5cm" svg:height="8.2cm" draw:style-name="ImgFrameStyle">
        <draw:image>
          <office:binary-data></office:binary-data>
        </draw:image>
      </draw:frame>
    </text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="ImageCaption">Figure: ShiftLine Work Orders &amp; Manpower Allocation Full Command Interface</text:p>
  

  <text:p text:style-name="BodyBold">Why Work Order Scheduling Demands Manpower Balancing:</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Eliminating Scheduling in a Vacuum: </text:span>Planners previously assigned 15 work orders to a Tuesday night without knowing whether 4 or 12 technicians were on duty, causing frantic midnight cancellations.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Dynamic Resource Consumption: </text:span>Each work order specifies its required crew size (e.g., 2, 3, or 4 staff). ShiftLine aggregates demand by shift and compares it directly against the live roster.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Real-Time Deficit Warnings: </text:span>If a shift requires 18 technicians but only 10 are rostered, ShiftLine instantly flags an 8-person deficit with an overload badge (e.g., 180% Load).</text:p>

<text:p text:style-name="PageBreak"/>

  
    <text:p text:style-name="SectionTag">SECTION III · MANDATORY WORK ORDER COLUMNS</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageTitle">18 · Mandatory Work Order Columns &amp; Dictionary</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageSubtitle">The Yellow Headings standard for seamless Maximo data mapping</text:p>
  
  
  <text:p text:style-name="BodyText">To ensure zero-error data ingestion from Alstom Maximo exports, ShiftLine enforces the standardized &quot;Yellow Headings&quot; data dictionary:</text:p>

  
    <table:table table:name="Table_ixcb7m" table:style-name="CustomDataTable">
      <table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_3_2"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_3_8"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_2_0"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_8_5"/>
      <table:table-header-rows><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Column Name</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Expected Format</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Mandatory?</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Description &amp; Operational Significance</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row></table:table-header-rows>
      <table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Line</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Text (&quot;Line 5&quot;, &quot;Line 4&quot;, &quot;Line 6&quot;)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">YES</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Defines the railway line and routes the order to the correct pool.</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Work Order #</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Alphanumeric (e.g. WO-90421)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">YES</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Unique Maximo work order identifier for audit traceability.</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Description / System</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Text (e.g. Point Machine PM)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">YES</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Maintenance activity scope; determines required trade specialty.</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Scheduled Date</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Date (DD/MM/YYYY)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">YES</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Target execution date; aligns with the corresponding roster day.</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Shift / Window</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Text (&quot;Night&quot;, &quot;Morning&quot;, &quot;Evening&quot;)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">YES</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Designated execution window governed by track possession rules.</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Required Manpower</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Integer (e.g. 2, 3, 4)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">YES</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Minimum qualified crew size required to execute safely.</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Duration (Hours)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Decimal (e.g. 3.5, 4.0)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">YES</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Estimated track occupancy duration in hours.</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Status</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Enum (&quot;Completed&quot;, &quot;Pending&quot;)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">YES</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Live execution tracking status for maintenance sign-off.</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row>
    </table:table>
  

  <text:p text:style-name="CoverSpacerSmall"/>
  
    <table:table table:name="Callout_xx9jga" table:style-name="CalloutBox">
      <table:table-column table:style-name="ColFullWidth"/>
      <table:table-row>
        <table:table-cell table:style-name="CalloutBoxCell">
          <text:p text:style-name="CalloutTitle">YELLOW HEADINGS STANDARD</text:p>
          <text:p text:style-name="CalloutText">In Alstom master workbooks, these 8 columns are styled with yellow header backgrounds (#fef08a). ShiftLine&apos;s import engine automatically detects this styling and maps columns with 100% precision.</text:p>
        </table:table-cell>
      </table:table-row>
    </table:table>
  

<text:p text:style-name="PageBreak"/>

  
    <text:p text:style-name="SectionTag">SECTION III · WORK ORDER IMPORT ENGINE</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageTitle">19 · Work Order Import Workflow &amp; Validation</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageSubtitle">Sanity verification, error handling, and pool routing during import</text:p>
  
  
  <text:p text:style-name="BodyText">Importing hundreds of work orders requires robust error trapping to prevent corrupted data from distorting manpower capacity calculations:</text:p>

  
    <text:p text:style-name="ImageContainer">
      <draw:frame draw:name="Img_4fayff" text:anchor-type="paragraph" svg:width="3.8cm" svg:height="1.1cm" draw:style-name="ImgFrameStyle">
        <draw:image>
          <office:binary-data></office:binary-data>
        </draw:image>
      </draw:frame>
    </text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="ImageCaption">Figure: Open Workorder Navigation Command</text:p>
  

  <text:p text:style-name="BodyBold">The 5-Stage Work Order Ingestion Pipeline:</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Stage 1: File Parsing &amp; Format Recognition: </text:span>Accepts .xlsx, .xls, and .csv formats. Reads raw data streams directly inside the secure browser sandbox.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Stage 2: Schema Validation &amp; Yellow Column Matching: </text:span>Verifies that all 8 mandatory columns exist. If column headers vary slightly (e.g., &quot;WO Number&quot; instead of &quot;Work Order #&quot;), intelligent fuzzy matching reconciles the difference.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Stage 3: Date Boundary Checking: </text:span>Validates that work order scheduled dates fall strictly within the active planning month. Out-of-bounds dates are isolated into a quarantined review list.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Stage 4: Manpower Sanity Bounds: </text:span>Verifies that crew requirements are realistic (between 1 and 8 staff). If a corrupted entry reads &quot;0&quot; or &quot;99&quot;, it defaults to 2 with a warning.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Stage 5: Pool Segregation: </text:span>Orders tagged &quot;Line 5&quot; route directly to the Line 5 database; orders tagged &quot;Line 4&quot; or &quot;Line 6&quot; merge into the Combined Pool.</text:p>

  
    <table:table table:name="Callout_icgruq" table:style-name="CalloutBoxSuccess">
      <table:table-column table:style-name="ColFullWidth"/>
      <table:table-row>
        <table:table-cell table:style-name="CalloutBoxSuccessCell">
          <text:p text:style-name="CalloutTitleSuccess">AUDIT SAFETY</text:p>
          <text:p text:style-name="CalloutText">Work orders imported into ShiftLine are permanently linked to their source file hash, enabling maintenance auditors to trace any assigned task back to its exact Maximo release batch.</text:p>
        </table:table-cell>
      </table:table-row>
    </table:table>
  

<text:p text:style-name="PageBreak"/>

  
    <text:p text:style-name="SectionTag">SECTION III · EXECUTIVE DASHBOARD: MONTHLY PLAN</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageTitle">20 · Executive Dashboard: Monthly Work Order Plan</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageSubtitle">Macro KPIs: Total Work Orders, Total Manpower, and Execution Health</text:p>
  
  
  <text:p text:style-name="BodyText">At the top of the Work Orders page, the Executive Dashboard synthesizes hundreds of individual work orders into high-level operational intelligence:</text:p>

  
    <text:p text:style-name="ImageContainer">
      <draw:frame draw:name="Img_hhtrfl" text:anchor-type="paragraph" svg:width="17.5cm" svg:height="7.6cm" draw:style-name="ImgFrameStyle">
        <draw:image>
          <office:binary-data></office:binary-data>
        </draw:image>
      </draw:frame>
    </text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="ImageCaption">Figure: Monthly Work Order Plan &amp; Work Order Status KPI Cards</text:p>
  

  <text:p text:style-name="BodyBold">Understanding the Core KPI Cards:</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Total Work Orders (Total WO: 316 Orders): </text:span>The gross count of all scheduled maintenance interventions across the network for the active month.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Total Manpower Demand (Total MP: 790 Technicians): </text:span>The cumulative sum of all required crew members across all 316 orders. Represents total technician-shifts required.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Average Crew Intensity (2.5 staff/order): </text:span>The ratio of Total MP to Total WO, providing immediate insight into maintenance complexity.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Completed Work Orders (120 Orders / 38%): </text:span>Work orders physically executed, verified by the shift supervisor, and signed off in the digital board.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Pending Work Orders (196 Orders / 62%): </text:span>Upcoming scheduled interventions remaining to be executed in subsequent shift windows.</text:p>

<text:p text:style-name="PageBreak"/>

  
    <text:p text:style-name="SectionTag">SECTION III · CREW INTENSITY FORMULA</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageTitle">21 · Crew Intensity &amp; Manpower Demand Formula</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageSubtitle">Mathematical modeling of maintenance task complexity</text:p>
  
  
  <text:p text:style-name="BodyText">A crucial metric surfaced by ShiftLine is Crew Intensity, expressed as &quot;(2.5 staff/order)&quot;. This chapter explains its mathematical basis and operational utility:</text:p>

  
    <table:table table:name="Callout_d0060c" table:style-name="CalloutBox">
      <table:table-column table:style-name="ColFullWidth"/>
      <table:table-row>
        <table:table-cell table:style-name="CalloutBoxCell">
          <text:p text:style-name="CalloutTitle">CREW INTENSITY MATHEMATICAL FORMULATION</text:p>
          <text:p text:style-name="CalloutText">Crew Intensity = ( Total Manpower Required ) / ( Total Number of Work Orders )\n\nIn our operational baseline: 790 Total MP / 316 Total WO = 2.50 Staff per Order</text:p>
        </table:table-cell>
      </table:table-row>
    </table:table>
  

  <text:p text:style-name="BodyBold">Why Crew Intensity Matters to Maintenance Directors:</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Task Complexity Benchmark: </text:span>A standard facility maintenance contract typically runs at 1.2 to 1.5 staff/order (mostly solo or pair technicians). A railway mainline maintenance contract averaging 2.5 staff/order indicates heavy, safety-critical interventions requiring gang work.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• High-Intensity Systems (3.5 – 4.5 staff/order): </text:span>Mainline rail grinding, turnouts and crossover replacement, overhead catenary tensioning, and substation transformer inspections. These require specialized gangs including track safety controllers, watchmen, and certified craftsmen.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Medium-Intensity Systems (2.0 – 2.5 staff/order): </text:span>Point machine preventative maintenance, axle counter calibration, and platform screen door drive belt replacements.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Low-Intensity Systems (1.0 – 1.5 staff/order): </text:span>Station CCTV visual inspection, passenger information display reboot, and emergency light battery testing.</text:p>

  <text:p text:style-name="CoverSpacerSmall"/>
  <text:p text:style-name="BodyBold">Resource Forecasting Table by Crew Intensity:</text:p>
  
    <table:table table:name="Table_9nidic" table:style-name="CustomDataTable">
      <table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_4_8"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_2_5"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_3_0"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_4_2"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_3_0"/>
      <table:table-header-rows><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Intervention Category</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Monthly Orders</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Average Intensity</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Monthly Technician-Shifts</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Primary Shift</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row></table:table-header-rows>
      <table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Mainline Permanent Way (Track)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">58 Orders</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">4.0 staff/order</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">232 Technician-Shifts</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Night Shift (N)</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Signalling &amp; Interlocking</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">112 Orders</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">2.5 staff/order</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">280 Technician-Shifts</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Night Shift (N)</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Station Platform Doors (PSD)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">86 Orders</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">2.0 staff/order</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">172 Technician-Shifts</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Evening Shift (E)</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">DCS &amp; Telecom Systems</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">60 Orders</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">1.8 staff/order</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">106 Technician-Shifts</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Morning Shift (M)</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row>
    </table:table>
  

<text:p text:style-name="PageBreak"/>

  
    <text:p text:style-name="SectionTag">SECTION III · RAILWAY SHIFT CLASSIFICATION</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageTitle">22 · Railway Shift Classification Engine</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageSubtitle">Safety-mandated rules for execution time windows and track possessions</text:p>
  
  
  <text:p text:style-name="BodyText">In a high-capacity driverless metro system like Riyadh Metro, maintenance tasks are strictly constrained by passenger revenue operations:</text:p>

  
    <table:table table:name="Table_zr53v" table:style-name="CustomDataTable">
      <table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_4_0"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_2_5"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_3_5"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_7_5"/>
      <table:table-header-rows><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Railway Asset Class</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Mandatory Shift</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Execution Window</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Safety &amp; Operational Rationale</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row></table:table-header-rows>
      <table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Mainline Track &amp; Switches</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Night Shift (N)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">01:00 – 04:30 (Possession)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Track access is strictly prohibited while passenger trains operate. Requires traction power isolation and physical track shorting.</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Signalling &amp; Axle Counters</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Night Shift (N)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">01:30 – 04:30 (Possession)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Testing switch machines and interlocking routes requires commanding points to move, which would trip active passenger train ATS safety circuits.</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Station Platform Doors (PSD)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Evening Shift (E)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">14:00 – 23:00 (Station)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Conducted during non-peak station hours when individual door leaves can be isolated without disrupting passenger flow.</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Station DCS &amp; Fare Gates</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Morning Shift (M)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">06:00 – 15:00 (Station)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Supervising technicians observe passenger transaction flow in real time to diagnose fare gate jams and network latency.</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row>
    </table:table>
  

  <text:p text:style-name="CoverSpacerSmall"/>
  <text:p text:style-name="BodyBold">The ShiftLine Classification Engine in Action:</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Automated Work Order Tagging: </text:span>When an order is imported, ShiftLine inspects the Description and Asset System. If it identifies keywords like &quot;Point Machine&quot;, &quot;Rail Grinding&quot;, or &quot;Catenary&quot;, it automatically binds the order to Night Shift.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Preventing Daytime Mainline Dispatch: </text:span>The system throws an immediate validation alert if a user attempts to schedule a track work order during Morning or Evening shifts.</text:p>

<text:p text:style-name="PageBreak"/>

  
    <text:p text:style-name="SectionTag">SECTION III · WORK ORDER STATUS TRACKING</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageTitle">23 · Work Order Status &amp; Execution Tracking</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageSubtitle">Real-time lifecycle monitoring: Scheduled, In-Progress, and Completed</text:p>
  
  
  <text:p text:style-name="BodyText">Tracking work order execution status in real time is vital for shift supervisors during morning and evening handovers:</text:p>

  
    <table:table table:name="Table_ker99v" table:style-name="CustomDataTable">
      <table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_3_5"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_3_5"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_5_5"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_5_0"/>
      <table:table-header-rows><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Work Order Status</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">UI Visual Indicator</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Operational Definition</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">System Action on Capacity Buffer</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row></table:table-header-rows>
      <table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Pending / Scheduled</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Amber Badge (Pending)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Work order is planned and waiting for its scheduled date and shift window.</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Deducts manpower from future shift capacity buffer.</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">In Progress</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Blue Pulsing Badge (Active)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Technicians are currently on site with active track possession or station permit.</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Locks assigned crew; marks capacity as actively consumed.</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Completed</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Green Check Badge (Done)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Work order physically completed, tested, signed off, and tools accounted for.</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Releases technician capacity; updates executive completion KPI percentage.</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Cancelled / Deferred</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Gray Strikethrough (Deferred)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Track possession was denied or spare parts unavailable; work order deferred.</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Restores technician capacity back to the available shift pool.</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row>
    </table:table>
  

  <text:p text:style-name="CoverSpacerSmall"/>
  <text:p text:style-name="BodyBold">Shift Handover Protocol &amp; Status Sign-Off:</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• 1. Physical Tool Reconciliation: </text:span>Before an in-progress night possession order is marked &quot;Completed&quot;, the shift lead must verify zero tools remain on track.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• 2. Single-Click Status Toggle: </text:span>The shift lead clicks the status badge directly in ShiftLine to transition the order from &quot;In Progress&quot; to &quot;Completed&quot;.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• 3. Digital Board Sync: </text:span>The update instantly reflects on the executive APSYS Digital Board banner, updating completion rates for senior management.</text:p>

<text:p text:style-name="PageBreak"/>

  
    <text:p text:style-name="SectionTag">SECTION III · SHIFT CAPACITY BUFFER CARDS</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageTitle">24 · Shift Capacity &amp; Net Resource Buffer Cards</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageSubtitle">Real-time calculation: Available Staff vs Required Manpower</text:p>
  
  
  <text:p text:style-name="BodyText">The core breakthrough of ShiftLine is the Shift Capacity Buffer Card, displayed for every day across all three operational shifts:</text:p>

  
    <text:p text:style-name="ImageContainer">
      <draw:frame draw:name="Img_r1z8j" text:anchor-type="paragraph" svg:width="17.5cm" svg:height="7.8cm" draw:style-name="ImgFrameStyle">
        <draw:image>
          <office:binary-data></office:binary-data>
        </draw:image>
      </draw:frame>
    </text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="ImageCaption">Figure: Shift Capacity Buffer Cards: Morning, Evening, and Night Manpower Balance</text:p>
  

  <text:p text:style-name="BodyBold">Anatomy of the Net Buffer Calculation Formula:</text:p>
  
    <table:table table:name="Callout_lxtcu" table:style-name="CalloutBoxWarning">
      <table:table-column table:style-name="ColFullWidth"/>
      <table:table-row>
        <table:table-cell table:style-name="CalloutBoxWarningCell">
          <text:p text:style-name="CalloutTitleWarning">NET RESOURCE BUFFER FORMULA</text:p>
          <text:p text:style-name="CalloutText">Net Resource Buffer = ( Rostered Technicians on Duty ) - ( Sum of Manpower for Scheduled Orders )\n\nExample Night Shift: 8 Rostered Staff - 65 Required Staff = -57 DEFICIT (813% Load)</text:p>
        </table:table-cell>
      </table:table-row>
    </table:table>
  

  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Top Metric: Available Technicians: </text:span>Derived dynamically from the active Roster Grid. Reflects the exact number of technicians scheduled on duty for that specific date and shift.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Middle Metric: Required Manpower: </text:span>Calculated by summing the &quot;Required Manpower&quot; column of all work orders scheduled for that date and shift.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Bottom Pill: Net Resource Buffer: </text:span>Displays the final operational margin. A positive buffer represents spare capacity for emergency corrective work. A negative buffer indicates an unworkable deficit.</text:p>

<text:p text:style-name="PageBreak"/>

  
    <text:p text:style-name="SectionTag">SECTION III · BUFFER DEFICITS &amp; OVERLOAD ANALYSIS</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageTitle">25 · Buffer Deficits &amp; Overload Capacity Analysis</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageSubtitle">Understanding severe bottlenecks: 129%, 200%, and 813% Load</text:p>
  
  
  <text:p text:style-name="BodyText">When maintenance demand exceeds rostered supply, ShiftLine generates high-visibility Overload Badges to demand supervisor intervention:</text:p>

  
    <table:table table:name="Table_rbmsj" table:style-name="CustomDataTable">
      <table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_3_0"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_4_5"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_2_2"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_8_0"/>
      <table:table-header-rows><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Overload Badge</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Operational Scenario</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Load Ratio</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Underlying Cause &amp; Recommended Mitigation</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row></table:table-header-rows>
      <table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">129% Load</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Morning Shift (7 Available vs 9 Required)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">1.29x Demand</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Minor deficit. Two technicians from General Shift (GS) can be reassigned to Morning Shift.</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">200% Load</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Evening Shift (6 Available vs 12 Required)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">2.00x Demand</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Moderate bottleneck. Stacking of station door inspections. Reschedule non-critical orders to adjacent days.</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">813% Load (CRITICAL)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Night Shift (8 Available vs 65 Required)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">8.13x Demand</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Extreme crisis. 16 separate mainline work orders scheduled on a single night. Physically impossible to execute. Mandatory redistribution required.</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row>
    </table:table>
  

  <text:p text:style-name="CoverSpacerSmall"/>
  <text:p text:style-name="BodyBold">Why the &quot;813% Load&quot; Crisis Occurs in Legacy Planning:</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Batch Calendar Stacking: </text:span>Maximo automated PM generators frequently schedule all monthly track maintenance on the &quot;15th&quot; of each month without checking resource availability.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• The ShiftLine Preventive Shield: </text:span>Without ShiftLine, this 813% load would go undetected until night dispatch, resulting in 12 cancelled track possessions and severe Alstom SLA penalty fines.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Resolution Action: </text:span>The planner uses ShiftLine&apos;s drag-and-drop timeline to distribute the 16 orders evenly across Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, and Thursday nights.</text:p>

<text:p text:style-name="PageBreak"/>

  
    <text:p text:style-name="SectionTag">SECTION III · DAY-BY-DAY TIMELINE NAVIGATION</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageTitle">26 · Day-by-Day Timeline &amp; Bottleneck Navigation</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageSubtitle">Selecting dates, inspecting daily loads, and balancing monthly capacity</text:p>
  
  
  <text:p text:style-name="BodyText">The Day-by-Day Timeline allows planners to navigate through the entire 31-day month, inspecting daily capacity health at a glance:</text:p>

  
    <table:table table:name="Table_yrx697" table:style-name="CustomDataTable">
      <table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_3_5"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_3_5"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_5_5"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_5_0"/>
      <table:table-header-rows><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Timeline Date State</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Visual Header Color</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Capacity Condition</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Supervisor Action Protocol</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row></table:table-header-rows>
      <table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Green Date (Balanced)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Soft Emerald (#10b981)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Net buffer is positive (+2 or more staff in every shift).</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Roster and work orders approved. No modifications required.</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Amber Date (Tight)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Soft Yellow (#f59e0b)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Net buffer is exactly zero in one shift.</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Approve with caution. Advise lead technician that zero absenteeism is tolerated.</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Red Date (Critical Deficit)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Bright Crimson (#e11d48)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Net buffer is negative (overload badge active).</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Action required: Reassign technicians from GS or reschedule work orders.</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row>
    </table:table>
  

  <text:p text:style-name="CoverSpacerSmall"/>
  <text:p text:style-name="BodyBold">Timeline Navigation Controls &amp; Features:</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Date Selector Slider: </text:span>Click any day button (1 through 31) to immediately focus the capacity cards and work order table on that specific date.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Bottleneck Filter Toggle: </text:span>Click &quot;Show Only Deficit Days&quot; to hide all balanced days and focus exclusively on dates with red overload badges.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Quick Shift Filter (M, E, N): </text:span>Isolate work orders belonging exclusively to a single shift window for rapid dispatch briefing.</text:p>

<text:p text:style-name="PageBreak"/>

  
    <text:p text:style-name="SectionTag">SECTION III · COMBINED POOL OPERATIONS</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageTitle">27 · Cross-Line Combined Pool Operations (L4 &amp; L6)</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageSubtitle">Managing shared multi-skilled technicians across Lines 4 and 6</text:p>
  
  
  <text:p text:style-name="BodyText">The Combined Pool for Lines 4 &amp; 6 presents unique operational opportunities and challenges that ShiftLine is specifically engineered to manage:</text:p>

  
    <table:table table:name="Table_skjsvo" table:style-name="CustomDataTable">
      <table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_4_0"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_4_0"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_4_0"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_5_5"/>
      <table:table-header-rows><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Operational Feature</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Line 4 (Orange Line)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Line 6 (Purple Line)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Combined Pool Advantage</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row></table:table-header-rows>
      <table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Route Length &amp; Stations</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">29.6 km (16 Stations)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">30.0 km (8 Stations)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Total 59.6 km monitored by unified roving maintenance gangs.</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Depot Location</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Depot 4 (Shared Facility)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Depot 6 (Shared Facility)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Common tool stores, spare parts warehouse, and dispatch offices.</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Technician Fleet</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">39 Multi-Skilled Technicians</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">39 Multi-Skilled Technicians</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Zero idle time: staff shift dynamically to whichever line holds active possession.</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row>
    </table:table>
  

  <text:p text:style-name="CoverSpacerSmall"/>
  <text:p text:style-name="BodyBold">Cross-Line Dispatch Rules in ShiftLine:</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Simultaneous Possession Balancing: </text:span>When both Line 4 and Line 6 schedule track possessions on the same night, ShiftLine computes the combined night demand against the 39 available staff.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Unified Certification Tracking: </text:span>All 39 technicians hold track access certifications valid on both lines, allowing seamless roster interchanging without safety compliance violations.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Depot Departure Staging: </text:span>ShiftLine tags each assigned crew with their departure staging depot to ensure on-time transit to remote station work sites.</text:p>

<text:p text:style-name="PageBreak"/>

  
    <text:p text:style-name="SectionTag">SECTION III · WORK ORDER EXPLORER &amp; FILTERING</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageTitle">28 · Work Order Explorer, Filtering &amp; Search</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageSubtitle">Advanced multi-column filtering, search, and dynamic view customization</text:p>
  
  
  <text:p text:style-name="BodyText">The lower section of the Work Orders module contains the Work Order Explorer, an enterprise-grade table for searching, filtering, and sorting hundreds of maintenance orders:</text:p>

  <text:p text:style-name="BodyBold">Explorer Search &amp; Filtering Capabilities:</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Global Instant Search Bar: </text:span>Search across all fields simultaneously by Work Order # (e.g. &quot;WO-90421&quot;), system keyword (&quot;Interlocking&quot;), station name, or technician name.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Multi-Dimensional Filter Chips: </text:span>Filter orders by Line (Line 5 vs Line 4&amp;6), by Shift Window (Morning, Evening, Night), by Status (Completed, In Progress, Pending), or by Trade System.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Multi-Column Sorting: </text:span>Click any table header to sort chronologically by date, numerically by required manpower, or alphabetically by system.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Column Visibility Customization: </text:span>Toggle optional columns such as Maximo Asset ID, Estimated Cost, Risk Level, and Equipment Tag.</text:p>

  
    <table:table table:name="Callout_1781ef" table:style-name="CalloutBox">
      <table:table-column table:style-name="ColFullWidth"/>
      <table:table-row>
        <table:table-cell table:style-name="CalloutBoxCell">
          <text:p text:style-name="CalloutTitle">BULK SELECTION ACTIONS</text:p>
          <text:p text:style-name="CalloutText">Planners can select multiple work orders using table checkboxes to perform batch actions: Batch Reschedule Date, Batch Shift Reassignment, or Batch Mark Completed.</text:p>
        </table:table-cell>
      </table:table-row>
    </table:table>
  

<text:p text:style-name="PageBreak"/>

  
    <text:p text:style-name="SectionTag">SECTION IV · CREW ASSIGNMENT &amp; LOCKING</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageTitle">29 · Hand-Picked Crew Assignment &amp; Manual Locking</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageSubtitle">Assigning specialist technicians and permanently protecting critical allocations</text:p>
  
  
  <text:p text:style-name="BodyText">While automated algorithms balance numbers, critical railway safety requires placing specific licensed specialists on high-risk tasks:</text:p>

  
    <table:table table:name="Table_7k4sa8" table:style-name="CustomDataTable">
      <table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_4_5"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_4_0"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_5_0"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_4_0"/>
      <table:table-header-rows><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">High-Risk Maintenance Intervention</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Required Technician Specialty</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Licensing / Certification Requirement</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Locking Action</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row></table:table-header-rows>
      <table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Mainline Point Machine Replacement</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Signalling Master Technician</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Alstom Level-3 Interlocking Certification</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Lock assigned technician in ShiftLine.</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Catenary Neutral Section Overhaul</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">High-Voltage Traction Specialist</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Traction Power Isolation Officer (TPIO)</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Lock assigned technician in ShiftLine.</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Axle Counter Head Replacement</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Telecom &amp; Signalling Tech</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Alstom Wayside Safety Sign-Off License</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Lock assigned technician in ShiftLine.</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row>
    </table:table>
  

  <text:p text:style-name="CoverSpacerSmall"/>
  <text:p text:style-name="BodyBold">How Manual Locking Works in ShiftLine:</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Hand-Picking Crew Members: </text:span>Clicking &quot;Assign Crew&quot; on any work order opens the technician picker, displaying qualified on-duty staff.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Engaging the Padlock Icon: </text:span>Once assigned, the supervisor clicks the padlock icon on the work order row. The lock turns gold.</text:p>
  <text:p text:style-name="BulletText"><text:span text:style-name="TextBold">• Absolute Protection: </text:span>Even if a user subsequently clicks &quot;Auto-Generate Roster&quot; or &quot;Auto-Balance Workorders&quot;, locked crew assignments are 100% immune from modification.</text:p>

<text:p text:style-name="PageBreak"/>

  
    <text:p text:style-name="SectionTag">SECTION IV · AUDITS &amp; MONTHLY CHECKLIST</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageTitle">30 · Data Exporting, Backups &amp; Complete System Audit Checklist</text:p>
    <text:p text:style-name="PageSubtitle">Operational readiness checklists, Alstom APSYS compliance, and ERP export</text:p>
  
  
  <text:p text:style-name="BodyText">To ensure world-class operational reliability, maintenance supervisors must complete the following standard operating checklist throughout the monthly planning lifecycle:</text:p>

  
    <table:table table:name="Table_28qsig" table:style-name="CustomDataTable">
      <table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_3_2"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_2_5"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_8_5"/><table:table-column table:style-name="ColCustom_3_5"/>
      <table:table-header-rows><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Planning Phase</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Timeline Target</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Mandatory Operational Verification Steps</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableHeadCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableHeadText">Sign-Off Authority</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row></table:table-header-rows>
      <table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Phase 1: Ingestion</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">D - 10 Days</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Import master Excel roster and Maximo monthly work order package. Verify 61 staff and ~316 orders mapped.</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Roster Planner</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Phase 2: Roster Audit</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">D - 7 Days</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Run Auto-Generate. Review Insights Panel. Resolve all shortfall errors using &quot;Show Who Can Cover&quot;.</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Shift Supervisor</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Phase 3: Capacity Balancing</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">D - 5 Days</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Audit Shift Capacity Buffer cards. Eliminate all red overload badges (813% / 200% loads) by redistributing PM dates.</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Maintenance Engineer</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Phase 4: Specialist Crew Lock</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">D - 3 Days</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Hand-pick and lock certified master technicians to high-risk night track possession work orders.</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCellAlt"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Safety Lead Engineer</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row><table:table-row><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Phase 5: Publication &amp; Export</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">D - 1 Day</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Export final PDF roster for depot notice boards. Push data to Alstom SAP HR and Maximo ERP.</text:p></table:table-cell><table:table-cell table:style-name="TableCell"><text:p text:style-name="TableCellText">Maintenance Director</text:p></table:table-cell></table:table-row>
    </table:table>
  

  <text:p text:style-name="CoverSpacerSmall"/>
  
    <table:table table:name="Callout_wk6gpn" table:style-name="CalloutBoxSuccess">
      <table:table-column table:style-name="ColFullWidth"/>
      <table:table-row>
        <table:table-cell table:style-name="CalloutBoxSuccessCell">
          <text:p text:style-name="CalloutTitleSuccess">APSYS AUDIT COMPLIANCE STATEMENT</text:p>
          <text:p text:style-name="CalloutText">ShiftLine complies fully with Alstom APSYS (Alstom Production System) Quality Standards for Maintenance Planning, ensuring 100% traceability, zero fatigue violations, and complete track possession safety certification across Riyadh Metro Lines 4, 5, and 6.</text:p>
        </table:table-cell>
      </table:table-row>
    </table:table>
  

    </office:text>
  </office:body>
</office:document>